STEPS=[
 [r'Use \((f+g)′=f′+g′\) and \((f-g)′=f′-g′\). Differentiate each term before combining.',r'(a) f′ = 3 and g′ = 12x + 2. Sum: 12x + 5. Difference: 1 − 12x.',r'(b) f′ = 34x and g′ = 2x/3 + 1/2. Sum: \(\frac{104}3x+\frac12\). Difference: \(\frac{100}3x-\frac12\).',r'(c) f′ = 3x² and g′ = 9x² − 8x. Sum: 12x² − 8x. Difference: −6x² + 8x.',r'(d) f′ = 12x² − 5 and g′ = 6x/5 − 2. Sum: \(12x^2+\frac65x-7\). Difference: \(12x^2-\frac65x-3\).','All functions here are polynomials plus constants, so these derivatives hold for every real x.'],
 [r'Use \((uv)′=u′v+uv′\), keeping both terms. For square roots, work with x > 0 and use (√x)′ = 1/(2√x).',r'(a) u = x² − 2, v = 3x + 1: y′ = 2x(3x + 1) + 3(x² − 2) = 9x² + 2x − 6.',r'(b) u′ = 28x³ + 2, v′ = 2x. Thus y′ = (28x³ + 2)(x² − 4) + (7x⁴ + 2x)2x = 42x⁵ − 112x³ + 6x² − 8.',r'(c) \(y′=2(\sqrt{x}-1)+\frac{2x-3}{2\sqrt{x}}=3\sqrt{x}-2-\frac3{2\sqrt{x}}\), for x > 0.',r'(d) \(y′=\frac{-3}{2\sqrt{x}}(4\sqrt{x}-2)+(-3\sqrt{x}+6)\frac2{\sqrt{x}}=\frac{15}{\sqrt{x}}-12\), for x > 0.',r'Check by expansion: (a) y = 3x³ + x² − 6x − 2; (b) y = 7x⁶ − 28x⁴ + 2x³ − 8x; (c) y = 2x√x − 2x − 3√x + 3; (d) y = −12x + 30√x − 12. Their derivatives agree.',r'Parts (c) and (d) are defined at x = 0, but their right difference quotients are unbounded there. No finite ordinary derivative is added at that endpoint.'],
 [r'Use \((u/v)′=\frac{vu′-uv′}{v^2}\), wherever the original denominator is nonzero.',r'(a) \(y′=\frac{3(x-4)-(3x-5)}{(x-4)^2}=\frac{-7}{(x-4)^2}\), x ≠ 4.',r'(b) u = −x² + 6x, u′ = −2x + 6; v = 4x³ + 1, v′ = 12x². The numerator is (4x³ + 1)(−2x + 6) − (−x² + 6x)12x².',r'Expand that numerator: −8x⁴ + 24x³ − 2x + 6 + 12x⁴ − 72x³ = 4x⁴ − 48x³ − 2x + 6. Divide by (4x³ + 1)²; exclude x = −∛(1/4).',r'(c) \(y′=\frac{5\sqrt{x}-(5x+6)/(2\sqrt{x})}{x}=\frac{5x-6}{2x\sqrt{x}}\), x > 0.',r'(d) Expand u = (2p + 3)(4p − 1) = 8p² + 10p − 3, so u′ = 16p + 10. With v = 3p + 2, v′ = 3, the numerator is (3p + 2)(16p + 10) − 3(8p² + 10p − 3) = 24p² + 32p + 29.',r'Thus \(f′(p)=\frac{24p^2+32p+29}{(3p+2)^2}\), p ≠ −2/3. The original denominator restrictions apply to every answer.'],
 [r'Find the point (a, f(a)), compute m = f′(a), and use y − f(a) = m(x − a).',r'(a) f(3) = 2 and f′ = 3. Therefore y − 2 = 3(x − 3), giving y = 3x − 7.',r'(b) a = −1/2, f(a) = −1/8, and f′(a) = 3a² = 3/4. Hence y + 1/8 = (3/4)(x + 1/2), giving y = 3x/4 + 1/4, or 3x − 4y + 1 = 0.',r'(c) f(2) = 1/5 and f′(x) = −1/(x + 3)², so m = −1/25. Thus y − 1/5 = −(x − 2)/25, or x + 25y − 7 = 0.',r'(d) f(3) = 3 and \(f′(x)=\frac{(x-2)-x}{(x-2)^2}=\frac{-2}{(x-2)^2}\). At x = 3 the slope is −2. Hence y − 3 = −2(x − 3), giving y = −2x + 9.',r'The rational functions are differentiable at the specified points; (c) excludes x = −3 and (d) excludes x = 2. Each tangent passes through its corresponding point.'],
 [r'The original equation requires x ≠ 0, y ≠ 0 and constant P ≠ 0. Rearrange: \(\frac1y=\frac1P-\frac1x=\frac{x-P}{Px}\).',r'Invert to obtain \(y=\frac{Px}{x-P}\). The additional restriction x ≠ P is necessary: at x = P no finite y satisfies the original equation.',r'Treat P as constant. The quotient rule gives \(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{(x-P)P-Px}{(x-P)^2}=\frac{-P^2}{(x-P)^2}\).',r'Check by rewriting y = P + P²/(x − P). Its derivative is again −P²/(x − P)².',r'The derivative is negative for real nonzero P and allowable x. It describes the change of image distance per change of object distance within this model; apply any physical distance/sign restrictions separately.']
]
ANSWERS=[r'(a) 12x + 5; 1 − 12x. (b) \(\frac{104}3x+\frac12\); \(\frac{100}3x-\frac12\). (c) 12x² − 8x; −6x² + 8x. (d) \(12x^2+\frac65x-7\); \(12x^2-\frac65x-3\). Each pair lists sum then difference.',r'(a) 9x² + 2x − 6; (b) 42x⁵ − 112x³ + 6x² − 8; (c) \(3\sqrt{x}-2-\frac3{2\sqrt{x}}\); (d) \(\frac{15}{\sqrt{x}}-12\). Parts (c),(d): x > 0.',r'(a) \(\frac{-7}{(x-4)^2}\); (b) \(\frac{4x^4-48x^3-2x+6}{(4x^3+1)^2}\); (c) \(\frac{5x-6}{2x\sqrt{x}}\); (d) \(\frac{24p^2+32p+29}{(3p+2)^2}\). Use the original-domain restrictions stated in the steps.',r'(a) y = 3x − 7; (b) 3x − 4y + 1 = 0; (c) x + 25y − 7 = 0; (d) y = −2x + 9.',r'\(y=\frac{Px}{x-P}\); \(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{-P^2}{(x-P)^2}\), with P ≠ 0, x ≠ 0, x ≠ P.']
PROJECT={'id':'ex-3-2-project','qNo':'Project','question':'Measure a ladder of length L feet. Its bottom moves away from the wall at 6 ft/min. How fast does its top move down when the bottom is 6 feet from the wall?','steps':[r'Take x as the horizontal distance from the wall and y as the height of the ladder top. The rigid ladder has fixed length L, so x² + y² = L².',r'Differentiate with respect to time: \(2x\frac{dx}{dt}+2y\frac{dy}{dt}=0\). Thus \(\frac{dy}{dt}=-\frac{x}{y}\frac{dx}{dt}\).',r'At x = 6 feet and dx/dt = 6 ft/min, \(y=\sqrt{L^2-36}\) and \(\frac{dy}{dt}=\frac{-36}{\sqrt{L^2-36}}\) ft/min.',r'The negative sign means the height decreases. The downward speed is \(\frac{36}{\sqrt{L^2-36}}\) ft/min. A numerical answer requires the measured L > 6 feet; L = 6 is a degenerate horizontal position, where this finite-rate formula is undefined.'],'answer':r'Downward speed \(\frac{36}{\sqrt{L^2-36}}\) ft/min, for measured length L > 6 ft.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','solutionStatus':'worked and checked','sourceReferences':[{'page':76,'jpg':'042_page_2.jpg','verification':'visually compared with JPG and full PDF'}]}
